Presidential elections were held in [[Benin]] on 13 March 2011 after being postponed twice from 27 February and 6 March 2011.<ref>{{Cite news|date=2011-03-05|title=Benin delays presidential election for second time|language=en-GB|work=BBC News|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-africa-12654718|access-date=2023-01-05}}</ref> Incumbent President [[Yayi Boni]] ran for re-election against thirteen other candidates, including former [[National Assembly of Benin|National Assembly]] head and political veteran [[Adrien Houngbédji]] and [[Abdoulaye Bio-Tchané]], president of the [[West African Development Bank]].<ref>[http://www.nation.co.ke/News/africa/-/1066/1105602/-/12hp419/-/ Benin postpones presidential vote to March 6] The Nation, 11 February 2011</ref> He won 53.18% of the vote, enough to win a second term without a run-off. It is the first time since the restoration of democracy in Benin that a candidate has won the presidency in a single round. A [[Two-round system|second round run-off]] would have been held on 27 March 2011 if it had been necessary.<ref>{{Deprecated archive|url=https://archive.today/20130124202914/http://www.google.com/hostednews/afp/article/ALeqM5hnjPTXROutdXTscwaJ3aldKuFGSw?docId=CNG.b805aff6c27f0198bb74cd3263c88566.4b1|title=Benin votes for president in twice-delayed election}} AFP, 12 March 2011</ref>

==Candidates==
===Adrien Houngbédji===
[[Adrien Houngbédji]], leader of the [[Democratic Renewal Party (Benin)|Democratic Renewal Party]] and runner-up to Boni in 2006, pledged to increase employment in the country's agriculture sector by investing 14 billion [[CFA francs]] ($28 million) in buying tractors and other heavy equipment for Beninese farmers. He also pledged to create an agricultural bank and lower the national income tax.<ref>{{Cite news|date=2011-01-13|title=Benin Opposition Candidate Pledges to Focus Policy on Farming|language=en|work=Bloomberg.com|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2011-01-13/benin-opposition-candidate-pledges-to-focus-policy-on-farming|access-date=2023-01-05}}</ref>

